# Error Handling Rules

## Package: @repo/error

All error handling uses `@repo/error` package with:
- **Centralized catalog** - All error codes defined in package, pre-registered at build time
- **Sentry integration** - Automatic error capture with PII scrubbing
- **Two-track handling** - Real errors to Sentry, safe messages to users
- **Framework-native** - Fastify handlers, React Error Boundaries, Next.js error pages

## Export Structure

The package provides platform-specific exports (no root exports):
- `@repo/error/core` - Core functionality (types, catalog, utils) - NO Sentry
- `@repo/error/node` - Node.js/Fastify (uses `@sentry/node`)
- `@repo/error/nextjs` - Next.js (uses `@sentry/nextjs`) - works for both client and server
- `@repo/error/browser` - Browser frameworks (uses `@sentry/browser`)
- `@repo/error/react` - React components

**Import Rules:**
- All imports must use platform-specific paths (no root `@repo/error` import)
- Type imports (`CatalogError`, `CoreErrorCode`) → Use platform-specific path or `/core`
- Runtime imports (`captureError`, `initSentry`) → Use `/node`, `/nextjs`, or `/browser`
- Core utilities (`getError`, `mapHttpStatusToErrorCode`, `getErrorMessage`) → Available from all platform exports

## Core Patterns

### Capture Error (Most Common)

```typescript
// For Next.js apps
import { captureError } from '@repo/error/nextjs'
// For Node.js/Fastify apps
// import { captureError } from '@repo/error/node'

const catalogError = captureError({
  code: 'NETWORK_ERROR', // ✅ Autocomplete for all catalog codes (CoreErrorCode | string)
  error, // Real error → Sentry
  label: 'API Call',
  data: { endpoint: '/api/data' }, // Internal context
  tags: { app: 'web' }, // ✅ Autocomplete for known apps ('api' | 'web' | 'mobile' | 'docs' | string)
})
// Returns: { code: 'NETWORK_ERROR', message: 'A network error occurred' }
```

**Type Safety:**
- `code` accepts `CoreErrorCode` (union type) for autocomplete and type checking
- `code` also accepts `string` for flexibility
- `tags.app` accepts known apps with autocomplete, or `string` for custom apps

### Extract Error Message

```typescript
import { getErrorMessage } from '@repo/error/nextjs' // or '/node', '/browser', '/core'
const message = getErrorMessage(error) // Type-safe
```

## Framework Integration

See framework-specific rules for integration patterns:
- [Fastify Rules](../backend/fastify.mdc) - Error handler plugin pattern
- [Next.js Rules](../frontend/nextjs.mdc) - Error pages and server actions
- [React Rules](../frontend/react.mdc) - Error boundaries

**Initialization** (idempotent - safe to call multiple times):

```typescript
// instrumentation.ts (Next.js) or index.ts (Fastify)
import { initSentry } from '@repo/error/nextjs' // or /node

initSentry({
  dsn: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN,
  environment: process.env.NODE_ENV,
})
```

## Error Code Format

Must be `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`:
- ✅ `NETWORK_ERROR`, `USER_NOT_FOUND`, `AI_MODEL_ERROR`
- ❌ `network-error`, `NetworkError`, `NETWORK_ERROR_`

## Best Practices

### Do

- ✅ Use `captureError` for all error reporting
- ✅ Use `mapHttpStatusToErrorCode` in Fastify handlers
- ✅ Return safe catalog errors to users
- ✅ Include context in `data` field
- ✅ Import from platform-specific paths

### Don't

- ❌ Never use `console.*` (use `@repo/utils/logger`)
- ❌ Never expose internal error details to users
- ❌ Never call `captureError` without required fields
- ❌ Never import from root `@repo/error` (use platform-specific paths)
- ❌ Never try to register errors at runtime (all errors are pre-registered)

